{"purpose":"Record the evidence baseline of the four-repository architecture review that produced the hardening scope: what was examined, what was verified by execution, and the overall assessment.","content":"## 1. Method\n\nFour repositories reviewed: `eka-standard` (1,609 lines of normative prose, no code), `eka-core` (26,986 source lines across 15 packages), `eka-cli` (27,488 source lines, 57 command files), `eka-mcp` (8,635 source lines, 26 MCP tools). Each was read structurally, then claims were verified by execution rather than accepted from prose.\n\nVerification performed: `go build ./...` and `go vet ./...` in all three Go repositories (both clean); `go test ./... -count=1` in eka-core (green, 409s total); `go test ./... -short` in eka-mcp (green); `go test ./... -short` in eka-cli (red — five bootstrap failures, see req:git-discovery-unbounded-walk). Individual claims were then spot-checked at source: the non-atomic exchange writer, the code-graph freshness path, the MCP initialize handshake, the annotation keys, the capture classifier, and the vendored-standard copies.\n\n## 2. Measurements\n\n| Repository | Source lines | Test lines | Ratio |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| eka-core | 26,986 | 26,425 | 0.97 |\n| eka-cli | 27,488 | 25,977 | 0.94 |\n| eka-mcp | 8,635 | 11,012 | 1.27 |\n\nZero `TODO`/`FIXME`/`HACK` markers in eka-core, no `gofmt` drift, no reflection, and exactly two interfaces (each with five or more implementations, so neither is speculative). This is unusual discipline at this size.\n\n## 3. Assessment\n\n**Sound foundations.** The store is genuinely immutable: `object_payloads` is insert-only, hashes are content-derived, and the store API exposes no update path for payload rows. SQLite is configured with WAL, a single-writer read-only fast path, forward-only schema migration at version 4, and a documented busy-retry policy that openly records why the driver's busy handler cannot be relied on across processes. Determinism is real rather than claimed: maps are ranged and then sorted before serialisation, archive timestamps are zeroed, and digests exclude the manifest to avoid self-reference. Conformance is read-only, honouring the standard's own enforcement principle, with each rule a self-contained function and non-literal interpretations marked at the point of interpretation.\n\n**Where prose outruns code.** Three documented contracts exceed what the implementation delivers: exchange emission is called atomic but is not (`req:exchange-export-atomicity`); the code-graph cache is described as a cache but decides freshness by doing the work it should avoid (`req:codegraph-cache-effectiveness`); and the CLI is described as containing no domain logic while carrying roughly half the ecosystem's source and a bespoke classifier (`req:capture-drafts-cannot-publish`).\n\n**Where the standard contradicts itself.** The authoritative corpus states 1.1 in two files and 1.3 in three others (`req:version-surface-drift`). A reader told to trust `specification/` above all else has no way to resolve that.\n\n**Where the agent surface lags.** The MCP server pins protocol 2024-11-05 and echoes any client version back without negotiation; annotations use keys the specification does not define; there are no prompts, no resource subscriptions, and structured content is deliberately unused. Write safety is nonetheless best in class — canonical writes are gated by default, concurrency is optimistic with expected-hash checks, and refusals carry the full conformance report plus an explicit retry affordance.\n\n## 4. Deliberate decisions, not defects\n\nThree review observations were retracted after checking recorded decisions, and should not be re-raised as defects: staying on protocol 2024-11-05 (recorded survey decision); refusing to rewrite legacy Markdown drafts in `relate` (documented implementation decision); and the absence of `structuredContent` (deliberate, tied to the pinned baseline). Recording this matters because a reviewer who does not check decisions will file the same three findings repeatedly.","investigationSummary":"","conclusion":""}